// THREE CONCLUSIONS, and the paper states all three in section 8. The top-level move is a
// FRIENDLY AMENDMENT: the paper grants Williams's account of ethics as sense-making and denies
// that it licenses the disciplinary conclusion he draws from it. That is why the relation from
// the section 3 result to Williams's argument is an undercut and not an attack.
//
// #reported does the heavy lifting here, because a claim in this paper may be Williams's,
// Dewey's, a critic's, or the paper's own, and the map has no other way to say which.

# What the paper concludes {isGroup: true}

[What value inquiry requires]: What is required is less a shift from scientistic to humanistic conceptions of philosophy, than for philosophers working in value inquiry to better align their aspirations for theory with what it can actually deliver. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "abstract", source: "\"What is required is less a shift from scientistic to humanistic conceptions of philosophy, than for philosophers working in value inquiry to better align their aspirations for theory with what it can actually deliver.\"", note: "A comparative thesis whose two halves are the paper's two lines of argument: the first half is answered by the sections 1 to 3 result about disciplinary placement, the second by the section 5 case for middle-range theory."}
    <+ [Middle-range rather than grand theory]
    <+ [No overall answer to where philosophy fits]

[The debate is not deadlocked]: Analysis using the tools of transdisciplinary epistemology suggests that the debate between Williams and moral realists is not deadlocked after all. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 16", source: "\"Our analysis using the tools of transdisciplinary epistemology suggests that the debate between Williams and moral realists is not deadlocked after all.\"", note: "The paper's own hedge, 'suggests', is kept."}
    <+ [Williams's view better fits the evidence]

[Dewey is the nimbler thinker]: It is Dewey who is the nimbler thinker about what philosophy might become, due to his explicit emphasis on a unified, flexible and transdisciplinary approach to inquiry. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§8, p. 25", source: "\"it is Dewey who is the nimbler thinker about what philosophy might become, due to his explicit emphasis on a unified, flexible and transdisciplinary approach to inquiry\""}
    <- [Williams and Dewey share deep commitments]
    <+ [Dewey complements and partly corrects Williams]
    <+ <Dewey's account of inquiry is the more flexible>

# Williams's case, as the paper reports it {isGroup: true}

[Philosophy is a humanistic discipline]: Philosophy is to be viewed as part of a wider humanistic enterprise of making sense of ourselves and our activities. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§1, p. 2", source: "\"as part of a wider humanistic enterprise of making sense of ourselves and our activities\"", note: "Williams's vision, which the paper sets out in order to amend rather than to assert. That it and Williams's account of ethics hold each other up is recorded as a claim rather than drawn as a pair of edges, since as edges it would be a support cycle."}
    <+ [Importing scientific methods obscures philosophy]
    -> [No overall answer to where philosophy fits]

[Importing scientific methods obscures philosophy]: Importing scientific methods, or assumptions that philosophers should adhere more closely to scientific methods, ends up obscuring or misinterpreting philosophy as an activity, rather than shedding light on it. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3", source: "\"importing scientific methods, or assumptions that philosophers should adhere more closely to scientific methods, ends up obscuring or misinterpreting philosophy as an activity, rather than shedding light on it\"", note: "The paper says the conclusion is drawn 'for both reasons', so the two objections are convergent and each is drawn as its own argument."}
    <+ <Williams's objection from reflective writing>

<Williams's sense-making objection to scientism>: Williams's second and load-bearing objection to scientism in philosophy. #reported {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3", note: "Assembling the premises into a numbered structure is the reconstruction's work; the claims are Williams's as the paper reports them."}

(1) [Philosophy is about sense-making]: Philosophy, or at least those parts of philosophy Williams is most interested in, is about sense-making. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3", source: "\"philosophy (or at least those parts of philosophy Williams is most interested in) is about sense-making\"", note: "The scope restriction is Williams's own and is kept: the claim is not made of all philosophy."}
(2) [Sense-making is local]: Sense-making is to a significant degree interpretive, historically situated and local. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3", source: "\"Sense-making is to a significant degree interpretive, historically situated and local.\""}
    <+ [Local concepts cannot be replaced]: We need concepts and explanations which are rooted in our more local practices, our culture, and our history, and these cannot be replaced by concepts which we might share with very different investigators of the world. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§1, p. 2", source: "\"we need concepts and explanations which are rooted in our more local practices, our culture, and our history, and these cannot be replaced by concepts which we might share with very different investigators of the world\""}
-----
(3) [Importing scientific methods obscures philosophy]

<Williams's objection from reflective writing>: Williams's first objection to scientism in philosophy. #reported {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3"}

(1) [Philosophical writing needs to be reflective]: Philosophical writing needs to be reflective. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3", source: "\"philosophical writing needs to be reflective\""}
(2) [Reflection needs a style that engages the reader]: Reflection requires styles of writing in which the reader's sense of what is important is engaged, rather than ignored or kept at a distance. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3", source: "\"that reflection requires styles of writing in which the reader's sense of what is important is engaged, rather than ignored or kept at a distance\""}
-----
(3) [Scientific manners misrepresent philosophical writing]: Assimilating philosophy to the manners of the sciences obscures rather than illuminates what philosophical writing has to do. #reported {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "enthymeme", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3", note: "The paper states the shared conclusion of both objections but not the writing objection's own intermediate step; it is supplied because the objection does not otherwise reach that conclusion."}

[Williams's target is limited]: Williams's goal is limited to providing a principled account of why questions relating to human self-understanding are not best interpreted as questions that can be answered using scientific means. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3", source: "\"His goal is limited to providing a principled account of why questions relating to human self-understanding are not best interpreted as questions that can be answered using scientific means.\"", note: "Recorded because the paper is emphatic that Williams, unlike some critics of scientism, does not disparage either the aims or methods of science."}
    +> [Philosophy is a humanistic discipline]

[Williams accepts that some philosophy extends the sciences]: Some work in philosophy… quite properly conducts itself as an extension of the natural or mathematical sciences, and hence is not humanistic. #reported #conceded {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§3, p. 6", source: "\"some work in philosophy… quite properly conducts itself as an extension of the natural or mathematical sciences\"", note: "The paper calls this a slight tension with what one would expect Williams's answer to be. The gloss 'and hence is not humanistic' is the paper's, not Williams's."}
    -> [Philosophy is a humanistic discipline]

[Williams's position admits of a range of strengths]: Williams's language is oracular enough that he could be read as making a strong claim about philosophy's models, a much weaker one, or one of an indeterminate number of claims of a strength between these two extremes. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§3, p. 7", source: "\"or one of an indeterminate number of claims of a strength between these two extremes\"", note: "Kept because it sets the limit of what any reconstruction of Williams here can claim."}

[The paper does not make Williams more determinate]: The paper's interest is less in forcing Williams's views on the nature of philosophy into a form more determinate than he chose to express them, than in arguing for a conception of philosophical inquiry in which it is the research problem which should determine the method. #conceded {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§3, p. 7", source: "\"My interest here is less in forcing Williams's views on the nature of philosophy into a form more determinate than he chose to express them, than to argue for a conception of philosophical inquiry in which it is the research problem which should determine the method\"", note: "A scope limit the author sets on his own critique, in Govier's sense a counterconsideration."}
    <+ [Williams's position admits of a range of strengths]
    -> [No overall answer to where philosophy fits]

# The disciplinary framing, and why it has no answer {isGroup: true}

[No overall answer to where philosophy fits]: It is a mistake to think that there is an overall answer to the question whether philosophy belongs with the humanities or the sciences. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3", source: "\"it is a mistake to think that there is an overall answer to the question whether philosophy belongs with the humanities or the sciences\""}
    <+ [Method should follow the research problem]
    <+ [Philosophy is too various for one grouping]
    <+ [Whether a field is both humanistic and scientific is a substantive question]
    <+ [Distinguishing the humanities from the sciences is harder than Williams suggests]
    +> [The blunt categorical question is unhelpful]
    +> [Philosophy has no asymmetric right of critique]

[Distinguishing the humanities from the sciences is harder than Williams suggests]: Distinguishing the humanities from the sciences is more difficult than Williams's paper might lead us to think. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§2, p. 5", source: "\"distinguishing the humanities from the sciences is more difficult than Williams's paper might lead us to think\""}
    -> [Williams presupposes a clear division]
    <+ [The humanities now use methods once thought scientific]: Sense-making remains crucial for the humanities, but it's now generally agreed that this sense-making may include as integral parts a range of methods which might previously have been thought of as distinctively scientific, such as data mining and model construction. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§2, p. 5", source: "\"Sense-making remains crucial for the humanities, but it's now generally agreed that this sense-making may include as integral parts a range of methods which might previously have been thought of as distinctively scientific, such as data mining and model construction.\""}
    <+ [No consensus that science requires the absolute conception]: There is no consensus among either working scientists, or philosophers of science, that science requires us to commit to the absolute conception of the world, or that it is reasonable to expect scientific inquiry to converge on a single unified model. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§2, p. 5", source: "\"there is no consensus among either working scientists, or philosophers of science, that science requires us to commit to the absolute conception of the world, or that it is reasonable to expect scientific inquiry to converge on a single unified model\""}
    <+ [The sciences differ among themselves]: Palaeontologists view the world very differently from physicists, just as ecologists differ in their methods and ontologies from electrical engineers. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§2, p. 5", source: "\"it is evident that palaeontologists view the world very differently from physicists, just as ecologists differ in their methods and ontologies from electrical engineers\""}
    <+ [Importing scientific reasoning need not presuppose reductionism]: Importing forms of scientific reasoning into philosophy need not presuppose implausible reductionism, inattentiveness to relevant differences, or even neglect of features such as social power. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§2, p. 5", source: "\"importing forms of scientific reasoning into philosophy need not presuppose implausible reductionism, inattentiveness to relevant differences, or even neglect of features such as social power\""}

[Williams presupposes a clear division]: Williams presupposes that his reader will agree that there is a clear distinction to be drawn between the methods and aims of the humanities and those of the sciences. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§2, p. 4", source: "\"Williams (2006b) presupposes that his reader will agree that there is a clear distinction to be drawn between the methods and aims of the humanities and those of the sciences.\""}
    +> [Philosophy is a humanistic discipline]

[The paper does not settle the philosophy of science]: The paper does not resolve summarily the complex debates in the philosophy of science on which the difficulty of the distinction turns. #conceded {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§2, p. 5", source: "\"My point here is not to resolve summarily these complex debates in the philosophy of science, but to explain why distinguishing the humanities from the sciences is more difficult than Williams's paper might lead us to think\"", note: "A point raised and deliberately dropped; recorded rather than completed."}
    -> [No overall answer to where philosophy fits]

<The cross-classification objection>: The humanistic and the scientific may cross-classify, in the way Williams said the analytic and the continental do — as though one divided cars into front-wheel drive and Japanese. {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence", pinpoint: "§2, p. 6", note: "The paper puts this as something 'someone could object' for all that has been said so far, then asserts the conclusion in its own voice. Reconstructed as an argument so that the undercut it delivers has premises."}

(1) [Humanistic is fixed by subject matter]: Whether a discipline or topic area is a humanistic one is determined on Williams's account by whether its subject matter is forms of human sense-making. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§2, p. 6", source: "\"Whether a discipline or topic area is a humanistic one is determined on Williams's account by whether its subject matter is forms of human sense-making.\""}
(2) [Scientific is fixed by method]: Whether an area of scholarly activity should be classed as a science is determined not by its subject matter… but by features of the methods used. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§2, p. 6", source: "\"whether an area of scholarly activity should be classed as a science is determined not by its subject matter… but by features of the methods used\"", note: "The elision marks the paper's parenthesis, 'and certainly not by its subject matter being something other than human sense-making'."}
    <+ [Scientific methods generate convergence and knowledge]: Uncontroversial features of scientific method include that the methods allow competing interpretations or hypotheses to be tested with agreed ways of determining which are superior, and that the methods are apt to generate both reasoned convergence over time among competent inquirers, and an expanding body of knowledge. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§2, p. 6", source: "\"uncontroversial features include that the methods allow competing interpretations or hypotheses to be tested with agreed ways of determining which are superior, and that the methods are apt to generate both reasoned convergence over time among competent inquirers, and an expanding body of knowledge\""}
-----
(3) [Whether a field is both humanistic and scientific is a substantive question]: Whether a discipline or area of scholarly activity can be both humanistic in so far as it aims to shed light on forms of human sense-making, and scientific in that it is apt to generate reasoned convergence and an expanding body of knowledge, is a substantive question rather than one that can be determined by stipulation. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§2, p. 6", source: "\"Whether a discipline or area of scholarly activity can be both humanistic in so far as it aims to shed light on forms of human sense-making, and scientific in that it is apt to generate reasoned convergence and an expanding body of knowledge, is a substantive question rather than one that can be determined by stipulation.\""}

<The cross-classification objection>
    _> <Williams's sense-making objection to scientism>
    <+ [Williams on cross-classification]: Williams famously ridiculed the distinction between analytical and continental philosophy as a cross-classification, as though one divided cars into front-wheel drive and Japanese. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§2, p. 6", source: "\"as though one divided cars into front-wheel drive and Japanese\""}

<Method should follow the research problem>: Research problems are made rather than found, and once the problem determines the method there is no reason to expect a single answer about where philosophy fits. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§3, pp. 7–8"}

(1) [Research problems are not found ready-made]: The world does not contain research problems ready to be discovered. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§3, p. 8", source: "\"The world does not contain research problems ready to be discovered.\""}
    <+ [Problematisation brings a situation into view as a problem]: Much of the creativity and skill in the early stages of a research project is devoted to the process of problematisation, whereby an inchoate situation is brought into sharper view as a problem. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§3, p. 8", source: "\"Much of the creativity and skill in the early stages of a research project is devoted to the process of problematisation, whereby an inchoate situation is brought into sharper view as a problem.\""}
    <+ [How a problem is conceived decides what counts as relevant]: The way in which the problem is conceived decides what specific suggestions are entertained and which are dismissed; what data are selected and which rejected; it is the criterion for relevancy and irrelevancy of hypotheses and conceptual structures. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§3, p. 8", source: "\"The way in which the problem is conceived decides what specific suggestions are entertained and which are dismissed; what data are selected and which rejected; it is the criterion for relevancy and irrelevancy of hypotheses and conceptual structures\"", note: "Dewey, quoted by the paper; the first appearance of the Deweyan thread that section 7 returns to."}
(2) [Problems may be transdisciplinary]: Research problems may be framed within a single discipline, but may also be multidisciplinary, interdisciplinary, or transdisciplinary, where solving the problem often requires unifying or reconciling existing disciplinary methods into bespoke forms. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§3, p. 8", source: "\"transdisciplinary (framed in a problem-oriented way, and where solving the problem often requires unifying or reconciling existing disciplinary methods into bespoke forms)\"", note: "Compresses the paper's four-way list into one claim."}
-----
(3) [Method should follow the research problem]: The philosophical methods that it is most fruitful to adopt depend on the research question to be addressed. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3", source: "\"the philosophical methods that it is most fruitful to adopt depend on the research question to be addressed\"", note: "Drawn as support for the no-overall-answer claim rather than run together with it: the source separates the two sentences with 'Rather,' and joining them would present as one statement what the author wrote as two thoughts."}

[Philosophy is too various for one grouping]: We should not expect all the breadth of contemporary philosophy to fit comfortably within a single grouping of disciplines. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§3, p. 9", source: "\"We should not expect all the breadth of contemporary philosophy to fit comfortably within a single grouping of disciplines.\""}
    <+ [Good work differs across philosophy's subfields]: Good work in the philosophy of physics may look nothing like good work in the philosophy of literature. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§3, p. 9", source: "\"good work in the philosophy of physics may look nothing like good work in the philosophy of literature\""}
    <+ [Many areas of philosophy work on another discipline's questions]: Many areas of philosophy, including philosophy of law, philosophy of biology, philosophy of education, philosophy of art, and philosophy of medicine, reflect on the philosophical questions and challenges that arise for scholars and practitioners within another academic discipline. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§3, p. 8", source: "\"Many areas of philosophy, including philosophy of law, philosophy of biology, philosophy of education, philosophy of art, and philosophy of medicine, reflect on the philosophical questions and challenges that arise for scholars and practitioners within another academic discipline.\""}
        <+ [Their norms come from the first-order discipline]: The prevailing norms for what counts as good work, and appropriate methods, in these different areas of philosophy are influenced by features that are unique to the first-order discipline and are frequently interdisciplinary. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§3, p. 9", source: "\"the prevailing norms for what counts as good work, and appropriate methods, in these different\"", note: "The quotation stops at the page break; the sentence continues 'areas of philosophy are influenced by features that are unique to the first-order discipline and are frequently interdisciplinary'."}
    <- [Interdisciplinary philosophy tends to be bad philosophy]: Interdisciplinary forms of philosophy tend to be bad philosophy. #contested {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§3, p. 9", source: "\"these interdisciplinary forms of philosophy tend to be bad philosophy\"", note: "The paper raises this as 'one possible response to such a line of thinking' and rejects it; it is nobody's stated position in the text."}
        <- [Williams does not take that line]: That interdisciplinary philosophy is bad philosophy is not the line that Williams takes, either in his pronouncements about philosophy, or his own practice. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§3, p. 9", source: "\"this isn't the line that Williams takes either in his pronouncements about philosophy, or his own practice\""}

[A friendly amendment, not an objection]: The research-problem-led approach is less an objection to how Williams does philosophy, than a friendly amendment to how he talks about doing philosophy. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§3, p. 9", source: "\"it's less an objection to how Williams does philosophy, than a friendly amendment to how he talks about doing philosophy\"", note: "The paper's own name for its top-level move, and the reason the section 3 result undercuts Williams's inference rather than attacking his premises. It bears on the amendment's soundness because the leading exponent of the humanistic conception already practises it."}
    +> [Method should follow the research problem]
    <+ [Williams's later work draws on other disciplines]: Williams's later work often draws attention to the ways in which philosophical reasoning, to answer its research questions, needs to be informed by other disciplines such as history or law. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§3, p. 9", source: "\"His later work often draws attention to the ways in which philosophical reasoning, to answer its research questions, needs to be informed by other disciplines such as history\""}
    <+ [Williams's own practice showcases the point]: Williams's own philosophical practice showcases that the methods it is most fruitful to adopt depend on the research question, even if his metaphilosophy makes this less clear. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§1, p. 3", source: "\"a point that Williams's own philosophical practice showcases, even if his metaphilosophy makes this less clear\""}

# Whether the dispute with the realists is a standoff {isGroup: true}

[Williams's argument may be question-begging]: There is something question-begging about Williams' argument for a humanistic conception of philosophy. #contested {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§1, p. 4", source: "\"This might lead to the worry that there is something question-begging about Williams' argument\"", note: "A worry the paper raises about the position it is examining, and then answers; not the paper's own verdict."}
    _> <Williams's sense-making objection to scientism>
    <+ [Ethics and metaphilosophy support each other on both sides]: Williams's views about the nature of ethics both support, and are supported by, his broader humanistic conception of philosophy, while other philosophers' views of ethics as being akin to science… both support and are supported by their distinctive conceptions of philosophy. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§1, p. 4", source: "\"Williams's views about the nature of ethics both support, and are supported by, his broader humanistic conception of philosophy, while other philosophers' views of ethics as being akin to science (or to mathematics) both support and are supported by their distinctive conceptions of philosophy\""}

<Williams from the record of ethical theory>: Williams's argument from the non-vindicatory history of ethical theory to in-principle limits on convergence in value inquiry. #reported {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§4, pp. 10–11"}

(1) [Classic ethical texts remain useful]: Scarcely any moral philosophers think that the insights contained within texts such as Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics or Kant's Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals have been assimilated or superseded in a way that makes it no longer useful to return to these texts. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 10", source: "\"scarcely any moral philosophers think that the insights contained within texts such as Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics or Kant's Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals have been assimilated or superseded in a way that makes it no longer useful to return to these texts\""}
(2) [In science the earlier theory becomes a limiting case]: In the natural sciences the earlier theory can be understood as a special or limited case of the later. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4, p. 10", source: "\"the earlier theory can be understood as a special or limited case of the later\""}
-----
(3) [The history of ethical theory is not vindicatory]: The story that should be given of how philosophical debates have evolved is 'not notably vindicatory'. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 10", source: "\"the story that should be given of how philosophical debates have evolved is 'not notably vindicatory'\""}
(4) [Value inquiry is a form of sense-making]: Ethics and political philosophy are also fundamentally about sense-making on Williams's account, and this sense-making, just as in historical scholarship, is systematically underdetermined by the constraints posed by factual accuracy and sound deliberation. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 11", source: "\"Ethics and political philosophy are also fundamentally about sense-making on Williams's account, and this sense-making, just as in historical scholarship, is systematically underdetermined by the constraints posed by factual accuracy and sound deliberation.\""}
    <+ [No final convergence in historical scholarship]: There are principled reasons for thinking that there will not and cannot be final convergence in historical scholarship, even when all those within an academic debate exhibit the virtues of truth. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 11", source: "\"there are principled reasons for thinking that there will not and cannot be final convergence in historical scholarship, even when all those within an academic debate exhibit the virtues of truth\""}
    <+ [What makes sense reflects particular audiences]: What makes sense isn't something that is fixed for all time, but reflects the needs of particular audiences at particular times. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 11", source: "\"what makes sense isn't something that is fixed for all time, but reflects the needs of particular audiences at particular times\""}
    <+ [The virtues of truth underdetermine the account]: The combination of the facts and the intellectual virtues provide real constraints, but they systematically underdetermine what could count as a truthful and accurate way of making sense of a particular set of events. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 11", source: "\"The combination of the facts and the intellectual virtues provide real constraints, but they systematically underdetermine what could count as a truthful and accurate way of making sense of a particular set of events.\""}
    <+ [Williams grants there are plain truths about the past]: Williams accepts that there are facts and plain truths about the past. #reported #conceded {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 11", source: "\"Williams accepts that there are facts and plain truths about the past\"", note: "The paper stresses that the argument 'begins from premisses that are favourable to realists', which is why the concession counts towards the argument rather than against it."}
-----
(5) [Limits on convergence in sense-making]: There are important in principle limits on the extent to which we should expect inquiries that focus on sense-making to lead to convergence on a single truth, and limits too on the extent to which any convergence that does occur can be given a vindicatory explanation. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 10", source: "\"There are important in principle limits on the extent to which we should expect inquiries that focus on sense-making to lead to convergence on a single truth, and limits too on the extent to which any convergence that does occur can be given a vindicatory explanation.\""}
    >< [Ethical theory could yet become vindicatory]

<It is early days for ethics>: The realists' reply: little progress so far is not the same as no progress possible. #reported #contested {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§4, p. 10", note: "Parfit and Williamson, as the paper reports them. It grants the record and denies that the record licenses the conclusion, which is why it undercuts rather than attacks."}

(1) [Secular academic ethics is recent]: It is recent for ethics to be undertaken as an academic inquiry entirely outside of the limitations imposed by religious traditions. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4, p. 10", source: "\"how recent it is for ethics to be undertaken as an academic inquiry entirely outside of the limitations imposed by religious traditions\""}
(2) [Lack of progress is not inherent to philosophy]: Lack of progress isn't inherent to philosophy as a discipline; the problem has been that philosophy has not been done in a way that is precise enough to ensure progress. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 10", source: "\"lack of progress isn't inherent to philosophy as a discipline; the problem has been that philosophy has not been done in a way that is precise enough to ensure progress\""}
-----
(3) [Ethical theory could yet become vindicatory]: Someone could accept that ethical theory has made relatively little progress so far, but argue that there is every reason to think that it could do, given the sustained use of better methods. #reported #contested {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 10", source: "\"Someone could accept that ethical theory has made relatively little progress so far, but argue that there is every reason to think that it could do, given the sustained use of better methods.\""}
    <- [Hopes of assimilation misunderstand value inquiry]: Expressions of hope based on the assumption that method in philosophy could be assimilated either to mathematics or to the sciences express a misunderstanding of the problems that value inquiry exists to examine. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4, p. 10", source: "\"expresses a misunderstanding of the problems that value inquiry exists to examine\"", note: "Williams's answer to Parfit and Williamson."}

<It is early days for ethics>
    _> <Williams from the record of ethical theory>
    <+ [It is one thing to say the record is poor, another that it must be]: It is one thing to argue that the history of ethical theorising has not so far been notably vindicatory, another to claim that it could not become vindicatory. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 10", source: "\"It is one thing to argue that the history of ethical theorising has not so far been notably vindicatory, another to claim that it could not become vindicatory.\"", note: "The paper's own statement of the gap the realists' reply exploits, which is what makes the reply an undercut."}

<Williams's commitments hang together>: Seeing ethics as sense-making under conditions of ethical freedom shows Williams's characteristic commitments to be unified. #reported {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§4, pp. 12–13"}

(1) [Ethical freedom]: We are substantively free to set our own ends, and thereby generate our own values and correlative practical reasons. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 12", source: "\"we are substantively free to set our own ends, and thereby generate our own values and correlative practical reasons\"", note: "Fricker's formulation of what she takes to be Williams's most fundamental commitment. The paper never asserts ethical freedom in its own voice; in section 8 it stays conditional, 'if Williams and Dewey are correct to think that we do indeed have ethical freedom'."}
    <+ [Ethical freedom is Williams's most fundamental commitment]: Williams's account of sense-making, his account of reasons, and much else in his philosophy can be brought into focus by the realization that his most fundamental philosophical commitment is to ethical freedom. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4, p. 12", source: "\"account of sense-making, his account of reasons, and much else in his philosophy can be brought into focus by the realization that his most fundamental philosophical commitment is to ethical freedom\""}
(2) [Value inquiry is a form of sense-making]
