# The meta-problem, and the case for modesty {isGroup: true}

[The meta-problem must be faced within philosophy]: Where there are incompatible research programmes within a discipline, with no obvious or agreed to criteria to determine which is most fruitful, the meta-problem must be faced within disciplines too. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§5, p. 13", source: "\"there are incompatible research programmes within a discipline, with no obvious or agreed to criteria to determine which is most fruitful, the meta-problem must be faced within disciplines too\""}
    <- [Philosophers enjoy an asymmetrical freedom to critique]: Philosophers enjoy the asymmetrical freedom to critique forms of reasoning in other disciplines without needing to take seriously the ways in which philosophical research programmes fall short by other disciplines' standards. #reported #contested {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 14", source: "\"philosophers enjoy the asymmetrical freedom to critique forms of reasoning in other disciplines without needing to take seriously the ways in which philosophical research programmes fall short by other disciplines' standards\"", note: "The paper calls this comforting and self-serving, and attributes it to philosophers who have little knowledge of other disciplines. If it held, the meta-problem would not have to be faced inside philosophy at all."}
    <+ [The meta-problem]: The meta-problem, which is at the heart of all interdisciplinary and transdisciplinary inquiry, is how we should combine or reconcile different modes of disciplinary inquiry. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§5, p. 13", source: "\"how should we combine or reconcile different modes of disciplinary inquiry?\"", note: "The paper poses this as a question; restated as a statement so it can bear on other claims."}

<The case for reducing ambition>: A research programme whose ambition persistently outruns its progressiveness has reason to reduce its ambition, and for value inquiry that means middle-range theory. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§5, pp. 14–16"}

(1) [Ambition is generality and precision]: The ambition of a research program is a vector of its generality, and the precision of the answers it provides to its research questions. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 14", source: "\"the ambition of a research program is a vector of its generality, and the precision of the answers it provides to its research questions\""}
(2) [Progressive means a vindicatory history]: A research programme is progressive to the extent that its history is vindicatory in Williams's sense. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 14", source: "\"A research programme is progressive to the extent that its history is vindicatory in Williams's sense.\""}
(3) [Philosophy is ambitious without being progressive]: Philosophical research programmes tend to be ambitious without being progressive. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 14", source: "\"philosophical research programmes tend to be ambitious without being progressive\""}
    <+ [Philosophical debate rarely yields decisive reasons]: The back-and-forth of philosophical discussion – even when conducted at the highest levels of sophistication and rigour over an extended timeframe – rarely leads to decisive reasons to endorse one theory over another. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 15", source: "\"The back-and-forth of philosophical discussion – even when conducted at the highest levels of sophistication and rigour over an extended timeframe – rarely leads to decisive reasons to endorse one theory over another.\""}
        <+ [Progress is refinement and a better sense of the price]: Philosophical progress consists in developing more refined versions of the theories we started with, and a better understanding of the price to be paid for maintaining each particular position. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§5, p. 15", source: "\"philosophical progress consists in developing more refined versions of the theories we started with, and a better understanding of the price to be paid\"", note: "Lewis, as the paper reports him."}
    <+ [Philosophers' confidence in ambitious theory stays high]: Philosophers' level of confidence that the discipline should be organised around the construction of ambitious theories remains high. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 15", source: "\"philosophers' level of confidence that the discipline should be organised around the construction of ambitious theories remains high\""}
        <+ [Parfit claims a priori normative knowledge while granting he is not understood]: We can discover some normative truths merely by thinking about them… Most philosophers seem to reject my metaethical and other meta-normative beliefs. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "n. 4, p. 15", source: "\"we can discover some normative truths merely by thinking about them\"", note: "The elision joins two sentences of the same footnote, which the paper prints together precisely because the pairing is the evidence: the second continues 'Many of these other people don't even understand what I believe. When I talk to these people, we can't even disagree.'"}
    <+ [No other discipline shows this discrepancy]: No other discipline exhibits such a discrepancy between its level of progressiveness, and the ambition of the theories advanced. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 15", source: "\"No other discipline exhibits such a discrepancy between its level of progressiveness, and the ambition of the theories advanced.\""}
        <+ [Anthropology matches low progressiveness with low generality]: In anthropology, low progressiveness is matched by low generality. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 15", source: "\"in anthropology, low progressiveness is matched by low generality\""}
        <+ [Chemistry's ambition is buttressed by testability]: Chemists make more ambitious claims, but those more ambitious claims are buttressed by an ability to form precise hypotheses and to test them much more definitively than is ever the case in philosophy. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§5, p. 15", source: "\"those more ambitious claims are buttressed by an ability to form precise hypotheses and to test them much more definitively than is ever the case in philosophy\""}
(4) [Sustained mismatch is a reason to reduce ambition]: Sustained mismatch between the level of ambition and the level of progressiveness provides reasons to reduce ambition. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 16", source: "\"Sustained mismatch between the level of ambition and the level of progressiveness provides reasons to reduce ambition.\""}
-----
(5) [Middle-range rather than grand theory]: Value inquiry should be done in a way that is both interdisciplinary and more modest, aiming to construct theories of the middle-range rather than grand theory. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§8, p. 25", source: "\"I proposed a different way of doing philosophy, which is both interdisciplinary and more modest in that it aims to construct theories of the middle-range, rather than grand theory\""}
    <+ [Testable claims allow more reliable progress]: Bringing the generality and precision of the claims made within value inquiry more closely into line with the ability to test them will allow for a more reliable way of attaining whatever level of progress turns out to possible. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 16", source: "\"Bringing the generality and precision of the claims made within value inquiry more closely into line with the ability to test them will allow for a more reliable way of attaining whatever level of progress turns out to possible.\"", note: "Quoted with the source's own slip, 'turns out to possible'."}
    <+ [Merton made the same move in sociology]: Sociologists do better when they shift away from totalising theories that attempt to explain everything in society, to… theories of the middle-range, which are deliberately limited in their range and applicability. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§5, p. 16", source: "\"sociologists do better when they shift away from totalising theories that attempt to explain everything in society, to what he described as theories of the middle-range, which are deliberately limited in their range and applicability\""}
    <+ [Middle-range theory is more useful]: Middle-range theory is often also more useful for the purposes of understanding and improving our current situation than more ambitious theory. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 17", source: "\"Middle-range theory is often also more useful for the purposes of understanding and improving our current situation than more ambitious theory.\""}
    <+ [Middle-range theories are more rigorous]

[Middle-range theories are more rigorous]: Middle-range theories are often more rigorous than more abstract and general ones. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 17", source: "\"middle-range theories are often more rigorous than more abstract and general ones\""}
    <+ [Straining beyond the evidence makes reasoning less rigorous]: Straining for a greater degree of precision and generality than the evidence allows makes reasoning less, rather than more, rigorous. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§5, p. 16", source: "\"Straining for a greater degree of precision and generality than the evidence allows makes reasoning\"", note: "The quotation stops at the page break; the sentence completes 'less, rather than more, rigorous.'"}
    <- [The claim about rigour may seem counterintuitive]: The claim that middle-range theory is likely to be more rigorous may seem counterintuitive. #conceded {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 17", source: "\"I acknowledge that the claim that middle-range theory is likely to be more rigorous may seem counterintuitive\""}

<The counterexample objection to middle-range theory>: Philosophers hold a theory refuted by any successful counterexample, however remote from the actual world, and on that standard a middle-range theory is bound to fail. #contested {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§5, p. 17", note: "The paper reports this as what philosophers often assume, and answers it."}

(1) [A counterexample defeats a theory however unrealistic]: Philosophers often assume that a philosophical theory cannot be correct if someone can produce a successful counterexample to it, and that it does not matter whether the counterexample is a real-world or a highly counterfactual one. #reported #contested {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 17", source: "\"Philosophers often assume that a philosophical theory cannot be correct if someone can produce a successful counterexample to it, and that it does not matter whether the counterexample is a real-world or a highly counterfactual one.\""}
    <- [The demanding standard is what creates the mismatch]: It is the combination of the commitment to a very high degree of generality in theories, with a view of what it is for a theory to be good enough that is so demanding that no philosophical theories in fact meet it, which creates the mismatch that we looked to middle-range theory to help solve. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 17", source: "\"it is the combination of the commitment to a very high degree of generality in theories, with a view of what it is for a theory to be good enough that is so demanding that no philosophical theories in fact meet it, which creates the mismatch that we looked to middle-range theory to help solve\""}
(2) [There are many ways the world could have been]: There are just so many obvious ways that the world could have been, which would render a middle-range theory that works for us now inadequate, were they to obtain. #reported #contested {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 17", source: "\"there are just so many obvious ways that the world could have been, which would render a middle-range theory that works for us now inadequate, were they to obtain\""}
-----
(3) [Middle-range theory cannot be good enough]: A middle-range theory is not good enough, because circumstances that would defeat it are easy to describe. #contested {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "enthymeme", pinpoint: "§5, p. 17", note: "The paper gives the objection's premises and its upshot, 'philosophers tend to look down on middle-range theory', but not this conclusion in so many words; it is supplied because the two premises do not otherwise bear on the rigour claim."}
    -> [Middle-range theories are more rigorous]

<Adequacy for the cases we face>: A middle-range theory answers to the cases we actually face, so failing in remote circumstances is not a defect in it. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§5, pp. 16–17"}

(1) [Success depends on usefulness for the questions we have]: The success of a middle-range theory depends on how useful it is for the kinds of research questions we want to answer, rather than on whether it has shortcomings in some conceivable circumstances. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 16", source: "\"The success of a middle-range theory depends on how useful it is for the kinds of research questions we want to answer, rather than on whether it has shortcomings in some conceivable circumstances\""}
(2) [Adequacy is for the class of cases we face]: What matters is the theory's adequacy for the class of cases that we actually do face or are reasonably likely to face. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§5, p. 16", source: "\"What matters is the theory's adequacy for the class of cases that we actually do face or are reasonably likely to face.\""}
-----
(3) [Remote failure is not a deficiency]: Just as we would not think a tennis racket deficient if it proved useless in getting a cannonball or a grain of couscous over the net, we may be unworried if a middle-range theory would have implausible implications in circumstances widely different from those we in fact face. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§5, p. 16", source: "\"Just as we would not think a racket deficient if it proved useless in getting a cannonball or a grain of couscous over the net\""}

<Adequacy for the cases we face>
    _> <The counterexample objection to middle-range theory>

<The mismatch is evidence for Williams>: Which of the two research programmes the evidence favours is settled by asking which of them predicts philosophy's actual record. {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence", pinpoint: "§§1, 5, pp. 4, 16", note: "An inference to the best explanation. The paper states the premises and the conclusion in two places twelve pages apart and does not lay out the step between them; the arrangement is the reconstruction's."}

(1) [Philosophy is ambitious without being progressive]
(2) [Limits on convergence in sense-making]
(3) [Ethical theory could yet become vindicatory]
(4) [Philosophy's methods are weak at resolving its disagreements]: No other discipline suffers such a mismatch between the level of confidence of its competent practitioners that there are correct and highly general answers to its research questions, and the power of the methods adopted within the discipline to resolve disagreements between these same competent practitioners. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§1, p. 4", source: "\"no other discipline suffers such a mismatch between the level of confidence of its competent practitioners that there are correct and highly general answers to its research questions, and the power of the methods adopted within the discipline to resolve disagreements between these same competent practitioners\""}
