    +> [Williams's view better fits the evidence]

[Williams's view better fits the evidence]: It is Williams's humanistic view of ethics that better comports with the totality of the evidence. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§1, p. 4", source: "\"it is Williams's humanistic view of ethics that better comports with the totality of the evidence\"", note: "The paper's own framing, 'Looked at in this way', ties the verdict to the transdisciplinary perspective rather than asserting it flat."}
    -> [Williams's argument may be question-begging]

[Philosophy has no asymmetric right of critique]: Adopting a transdisciplinary epistemology, there's no reason to think that philosophy should have an asymmetric ability to criticise other forms of insight and methods of inquiry, while itself being immune from critique from these perspectives. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 22", source: "\"adopting a transdisciplinary epistemology, there's no reason to think that philosophy should have an asymmetric ability to criticise other forms of insight and methods of inquiry, while itself being immune from critique from these perspectives\""}
