# Cases, fiction and the arts {isGroup: true}

[The blunt categorical question is unhelpful]: Asking whether thought experiments in ethics are best understood as a way of doing science, or as part of humanistic inquiry, is a blunt categorical question, and such a question is unhelpful. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§6, p. 18", source: "\"such a blunt categorical question is unhelpful\"", note: "Section 6 applies the sections 1 to 3 result to a second-order case, which is why that result is drawn as an intermediate conclusion and not only as a contention."}
    +> [Cases may be treated as experiments or as narratives]

[Cases may be treated as experiments or as narratives]: There is no reason why it can't be fruitful in some circumstances to treat cases as scientific experiments, and in others to think of them as literary narratives. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§6, p. 18", source: "\"There is no reason why it can't be fruitful in some circumstances to treat cases as scientific experiments, and in others to think of them as literary narratives.\""}
    +> [Fecundity of response may be the sign of a good case]

<The method of cases>: Cases are contrived to isolate the factors under test, intuitions about them are gathered, and ethical theory is built and tested on the result. #reported {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§6, p. 18", note: "The practice the section examines, reported through Kamm; not the paper's own method."}

(1) [Real-life cases do not isolate what matters]: Real-life cases often do not contain the relevant, or solely the relevant, characteristics to help in our search for principles. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§6, p. 18", source: "\"Real-life cases often do not contain the relevant–or solely the relevant–characteristics to help in our search for principles\""}
(2) [Cases should be contrived to isolate factors]: If our aim is to discover the relative weight of, say, two factors, we should consider cases that involve only these two factors, perhaps artificially, rather than distract ourselves with other factors and options. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§6, p. 18", source: "\"If our aim is to discover the relative weight of, say, two factors, we should consider cases that involve only these two factors, perhaps artificially, rather than distract ourselves with other factors and options\""}
-----
(3) [Intuitions about cases drive theory construction]: Presenting thought experiments, gauging one's own and other philosophers' intuitions about them, and using this to drive progress in the testing and construction of ethical theories is a method that can deliver such progress. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§6, p. 18", source: "\"presenting thought experiments, gauging one's own and other philosophers' intuitions about them, and using this to drive progress in the testing and construction of ethical theories\""}
    +> [Philosophers treat thought experiments as science]
    <- [Williams used cases for something else]: In advancing and discussing his thought experiments, Williams clearly took himself to be doing something other than science, and was at pains not to use the cases as a way of deriving a straightforward judgement or counterexample. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§6, p. 18", source: "\"he is at pains not to use the cases as a way of deriving a straightforward judgement or counterexample\"", note: "Joins two sentences that sit about 280 characters apart in the source, the second describing how Williams handles George the Chemist and Jim and the Indians."}

<The science analogy tells against the method of cases>: Taken seriously, the comparison with experiment holds thought experiments to a standard of design and validity they have never been held to. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§6, pp. 19–20"}

(1) [The analogy with experiment is apt so far as it goes]: So far as thought experiments allow fine distinctions to be drawn between different kinds of cases, the analogy with scientific experimentation is apt. #conceded {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§6, p. 19", source: "\"the analogy with scientific experimentation is apt\"", note: "The paper grants the comparison before turning it round: thought experiments are 'unparalleled in their ability to raise points of principle, in at least a somewhat concrete form'."}
(2) [Good science attends to design and validity]: Good science requires rigorous attention to experimental design, and to the limits of the power of any particular experiment to rule out competing hypotheses. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§6, p. 19", source: "\"good science requires rigorous attention to experimental design, and to the limits of the power of any particular experiment to rule out competing hypotheses\""}
(3) [Thought experiments have had no comparable methodological attention]: Thought experiments have unfortunately not received anything like the same systematic approach to their design, methodology and real-world extrapolation as is found in other disciplines. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§6, p. 19", source: "\"Thought experiments have unfortunately not received anything like the same systematic approach to their design, methodology and real-world extrapolation as is found in other disciplines\""}
    <+ [The Beholder's Share challenges internal validity]: The Beholder's Share poses a methodological challenge for achieving internal validity in thought experiments. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§6, p. 20", source: "\"The Beholder's Share not only poses a methodological challenge for achieving internal validity in thought experiments\""}
-----
(4) [The science analogy makes thought experiments look less reliable]: The analogy with science may make thought experiments seem less, rather than more, reliable when we look across to the seriousness with which methodology is taken in other sciences. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§6, pp. 19–20", source: "\"seem less, rather than more, reliable when we look across to the seriousness with which methodology is taken in other sciences\"", note: "The sentence straddles a page break in the converted source, so only its second half is quoted."}

<The science analogy tells against the method of cases>
    _> <The method of cases>

<Experimental science on humans is middle-range>: If thought experiments really are experiments, then what experiments on human subjects actually deliver is what they should expect to deliver. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§6, p. 19"}

(1) [Rigorous human experiments rarely give clean results]: Even in cases such as drug development, where teams of hundreds or thousands work to design an intervention and then test it in a randomised clinical trial, it's rare rather than common that rigorously conducted scientific experiments lead to a drug that works in the experimental environment without unacceptable side effects. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§6, p. 19", source: "\"Even in cases such as drug development, where teams of hundreds or thousands work to design an intervention and then test it in a randomised clinical trial, it's rare rather than common that rigorously conducted scientific experiments lead to a drug that works in the experimental environment without unacceptable side effects.\""}
(2) [External validity problems are deep and persistent]: Even where an experiment is internally valid and results can be reproduced in an experimental environment, there are deep and persistent problems of external validity. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§6, p. 19", source: "\"Even where an experiment is internally valid and results can be reproduced in an experimental environment, there are deep and persistent problems of external validity.\""}
-----
(3) [Experimental science on humans yields middle-range theory]: Experimental science that takes human beings as its subject matter will typically lead to middle-range, rather than to more ambitious theory. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§6, p. 19", source: "\"Experimental science that takes human beings as its subject matter will typically lead to middle-range, rather than to more ambitious theory.\""}
    +> [Thought experiments as science support the shift to middle-range]

[Thought experiments as science support the shift to middle-range]: Conceiving of thought experiments in ethics as a way of doing science may support rather than undermine reasons for shifting to middle-range theories. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§6, p. 19", source: "\"conceiving of thought experiments in ethics as a way of doing science may support rather than undermine reasons for shifting to middle-range theories\"", note: "The section's reversal: the practitioners who claim the authority of science are the ones the analogy pushes towards modesty."}
    <+ [Philosophers treat thought experiments as science]: Philosophers often describe thought experiments in ethics as if they are doing science. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§6, p. 18", source: "\"Philosophers often describe thought experiments in ethics as if they are doing science.\""}
    +> [Middle-range rather than grand theory]

<Fecundity rather than homogeneity>: If cases are fictions the reader completes, and ethics is not aimed at convergence, a good case is one that generates responses rather than one that compels a single answer. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§6, p. 20"}

(1) [Thought experiments are short pieces of fiction]: Thought experiments themselves are short pieces of fiction that the reader needs to make sense of. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§6, p. 20", source: "\"Thought experiments themselves are short pieces of fiction that the reader needs to make sense of.\""}
(2) [The Beholder's Share]: The interpreter's response to a work of fiction, and to artworks more broadly, is always a creative one, in which the interpreter completes the work. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§6, p. 20", source: "\"The interpreter's response to a work of fiction (and artworks more broadly) is always a creative one, in which the interpreter completes the work.\""}
(3) [There is no single correct use of the Beholder's Share]: There are better and worse ways of making use of the Beholder's Share, but there isn't one single correct way. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§6, p. 20", source: "\"There are, of course, better and worse ways of making use of the Beholder's Share, but there isn't one single correct way\""}
(4) [Ethical freedom]
-----
(5) [Fecundity of response may be the sign of a good case]: If, like Williams, we are persuaded that we have ethical freedom, we may come to think that it is fecundity of response, rather than homogeneity, that is the sign of a good case. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§6, p. 20", source: "\"we may come to think that it is fecundity of response, rather than homogeneity, that is the sign of a good case\"", note: "The conditional and the hedge are the paper's: it says 'we may come to think', not that this is so."}
    +> [Art gives values and meanings words cannot carry]

# Dewey, and what Williams left undone {isGroup: true}

[Dewey complements and partly corrects Williams]: Other philosophers of ethical freedom have proposed accounts that explore the ethical potential of the arts much more fully and provide a good complement and partial corrective to Williams. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 21", source: "\"Other philosophers of ethical freedom have proposed accounts that explore the ethical potential of the arts much more fully and provide a good complement and partial corrective to Williams.\""}
    <+ <Dewey goes further than Williams on the arts>

<Dewey goes further than Williams on the arts>: Williams left the positive role of the arts at the level of hints; Dewey argued it out. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§7, pp. 21–22"}

(1) [Williams gives only scattered hints about the arts]: Williams gives little more than scattered hints about how the arts might play such a positive role. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 21", source: "\"Williams gives little more than scattered hints about how the arts might play such a positive role.\""}
    <+ [Williams's examples mostly undercut ethical theory]: Given the mostly critical tone of Williams's engagement in ethics, his use of examples from the arts serves mostly to undercut the pretensions of ethical theory. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§7, p. 21", source: "\"his use of such examples serves mostly to undercut the pretensions of ethical theory\""}
        <- [Williams grants the arts a role in developing valuing]: Williams accepts that the thick concepts and exemplars by which we navigate ethical life are local and human created, and would agree that the arts play a crucial role in articulating and developing novel modes of valuing. #reported #conceded {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§7, p. 21", source: "\"Williams, of course, accepts that the thick concepts and exemplars by which we navigate ethical life are local and human created, and would agree that the arts play a crucial role in articulating and developing novel modes of valuing.\""}
    <+ [Williams on what music can do that words cannot]: Music has the power to express feeling that cannot be fully captured in words, a power which underlies the special opportunities that opera possesses as a form of drama. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§7, p. 21", source: "\"the power of music to express feeling that cannot be fully captured in words, a power which underlies the special opportunities that opera possesses as a form of drama\"", note: "The clearest of the hints, from Williams's writing on opera."}
(2) [Art gives values and meanings words cannot carry]: There are values and meanings that can be expressed only by immediately visible and audible qualities, and to ask what they mean in the sense of something that can be put into words is to deny their distinctive existence. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 22", source: "\"There are values and meanings that can be expressed only by immediately visible and audible qualities, and to ask what they mean in the sense of something that can be put into words is to deny their distinctive existence\"", note: "Dewey, quoted by the paper."}
(3) [Taking art seriously challenges philosophical method]: Taking art seriously has the potential not just to act as an adjunct or extension to traditional philosophical methods in ethics, but to fundamentally challenge them. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 22", source: "\"taking art seriously has the potential not just to act as an adjunct or extension to traditional philosophical methods in ethics, but to fundamentally challenge them\""}
    <+ [Philosophy may look questionable from the side of art]: Philosophers forget that philosophy may look equally as questionable from the perspective of art or of immediate experience. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§7, p. 22", source: "\"They forget that philosophy may look equally as questionable from the perspective of art or of immediate experience.\""}
-----
(4) [Dewey explores what Williams only gestured at]: On the ethical potential of the arts, Dewey provides the extended argument for which Williams offers only hints. {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence", pinpoint: "§7, pp. 21–22", note: "The comparison the section is built to make; the paper states the two halves and the verdict of section 8, but not this intermediate step."}

<Williams's late work is pragmatist>: Williams's quarrel was with Rorty, and it does not reach the pragmatism Dewey stands for. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§7, pp. 22–23"}

(1) [Williams took Rorty to define pragmatism]: Williams took Rorty's position to define pragmatism for the purposes of his critique. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 22", source: "\"Williams took Rorty's position to define pragmatism for the purposes of his critique\""}
(2) [The tradition includes objectivists as well as subjectivists]: The pragmatist tradition encompasses both subjectivists such as James and Rorty and objectivists such as Peirce, C. I. Lewis, Ramsey, and Dewey. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 22", source: "\"the pragmatist tradition encompasses both subjectivists such as James and Rorty and objectivists such as Peirce, C. I. Lewis, Ramsey, and Dewey\""}
(3) [The anti-pragmatist arguments miss the objectivists]: Williams's antipragmatist arguments have some force against subjective pragmatists, but none against objectivist pragmatists. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 22", source: "\"Williams's antipragmatist arguments have some force against subjective pragmatists, but none against objectivist pragmatists\""}
(4) [Queloz reads the genealogy as pragmatic]: Williams's genealogy is a perspicuous derivation, from needs we have anyway, of the need for an intrinsic value of truth. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§7, p. 23", source: "\"The genealogy is a perspicuous derivation, from needs we have anyway, of the need for an intrinsic value of truth\""}
-----
(5) [Williams's late work belongs to the pragmatist tradition]: Notwithstanding his protests to the contrary, there is a good case for taking Williams's late work to be a contribution to the pragmatist tradition. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 23", source: "\"notwithstanding his protests to the contrary, there is a good case for taking Williams's late work to be a contribution to the pragmatist tradition\""}
    <- [Two elements remain in tension with Dewey]: Some elements of Williams's overall perspective remain in tension even with a Deweyan pragmatist outlook – namely the absolute conception of reality, and his insistence that there is a fundamental difference between scientific and ethical inquiry. #conceded {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 23", source: "\"some elements of Williams's overall perspective remain in tension even with a Deweyan pragmatist outlook – namely the absolute conception of reality, and his insistence that there is a fundamental difference between scientific and ethical inquiry\""}
    +> [Williams could have gained coherence by dropping them]

[Williams could have gained coherence by dropping them]: Williams could have increased the coherence of his position if he abandoned the absolute conception and the insistence on a fundamental difference between scientific and ethical inquiry. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§7, p. 23", source: "\"there are reasons to think that Williams could have increased the coherence of his position if he abandoned these elements\""}
    <+ [The absolute conception faded in Williams's own late work]: In Williams's late work, the role of the absolute conception was diminished still further. {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§7, p. 24", source: "\"In his late work, the role of the absolute conception was diminished still further\"", note: "The paper adds that in Truth and Truthfulness it is mentioned only once, in a footnote calling it controversial whether the idea is coherent."}
    <+ [Dewey's account would have helped Williams]: Adopting this Deweyan account of inquiry might have allowed Williams both to bring more sharply into focus what it would take to vindicate the absolute conception, and also made more perspicuous to him that doing good science is not dependent on such a vindication. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 24", source: "\"Adopting this Deweyan account of inquiry might have allowed Williams both to bring more sharply into focus what it would take to vindicate the absolute conception, and also made more perspicuous to him that doing good science is not dependent on such a vindication.\""}
    +> [Dewey is the nimbler thinker]

<Dewey's account of inquiry is the more flexible>: Dewey treats the reach of the local as an outcome of inquiry rather than a standing constraint upon it, which is what a transdisciplinary epistemology needs. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§7, p. 24"}

(1) [For Dewey inquiry has one structure across domains]: Dewey argues that inquiry has the same kind of structure across all domains, integrating experience, including aesthetic experience, reasoning and action. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 24", source: "\"Dewey argues that inquiry has the same kind of structure across all domains, integrating experience (including aesthetic experience), reasoning and action\""}
(2) [Deweyan inquiry is iterative]: Inquiry on the Deweyan picture is iterative, in that each new inquiry is able to draw on the problematisation, observation, reasoning and judgements of previous inquiries, and generates resources that can be used in future inquiries. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 24", source: "\"Inquiry on the Deweyan picture is iterative, in that each new inquiry is able to draw on the problematisation, observation, reasoning and judgements of previous inquiries, and generates resources that can be used in future inquiries.\""}
(3) [Whether to eliminate the local is settled through inquiry]: For Dewey, how far an inquiry should aspire to eliminate our own local ways of apprehending things is something to be clarified through the iterative process of inquiry, rather than something to be imposed on particular inquiries as a requirement from outside. #reported {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§7, p. 24", source: "\"something to be clarified through the iterative process of inquiry, rather than something to be imposed on particular inquiries\""}
    <+ [Iteration may push either way]: In some domains, epistemic iteration may push inquirers in the direction of eliminating what is peculiar to our local ways of apprehending things, while in others it may lead us to insist on maintaining the local and the contextual. #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§7, p. 24", source: "\"In some domains, epistemic iteration may push inquirers in the direction of eliminating what is peculiar to our local ways of apprehending things, while in others it may lead us to insist on maintaining the local and the contextual.\""}
-----
(4) [Dewey is the nimbler thinker]

[Williams and Dewey share deep commitments]: Both Williams and Dewey share a deep commitment to ethical freedom, to naturalism, and to defending a conception of philosophy that gives significant weight to experience, and to the arts. #conceded {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§8, p. 25", source: "\"Both Williams and Dewey share a deep commitment to ethical freedom, to naturalism, and to defending a conception of philosophy that gives significant weight to experience, and to the arts.\"", note: "The paper's own concession, introduced in section 8 by 'Despite these commonalities' — a counterconsideration in Govier's sense rather than a reason for the verdict."}

[The two focus on complementary things]: While Williams focuses on working through the crisis of legitimacy for our shared ethical values which the acknowledgement of ethical freedom brings, Dewey focuses in a complementary way on the potential of creativity to provide a transformative vision through which experience can be enriched. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§8, p. 25", source: "\"While Williams focuses on working through the crisis of legitimacy for our shared ethical values which the acknowledgement of ethical freedom brings, Dewey focuses in a complementary way on the potential of creativity to provide a transformative vision through which experience can be enriched.\""}
    +> [Williams and Dewey share deep commitments]

# What ethical freedom would mean for method {isGroup: true}

<What ethical freedom implies for method>: If we are ethically free, expecting ethical inquiry to converge and to vindicate its results is a misunderstanding of what it is. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§4, p. 12", note: "The paper keeps the antecedent conditional throughout, so the implications are drawn from the premise rather than asserted."}

(1) [Ethical freedom]
(2) [Limits on convergence in sense-making]
-----
(3) [Expecting consensus misunderstands ethical inquiry]: Even if there are other areas of philosophy in which precise articulation of claims, and rigorous argumentation could plausibly lead not only to a consensus, but to a vindicatory explanation of this consensus, we misunderstand the nature of ethical inquiry if we expect this. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 12", source: "\"Even if there are other areas of philosophy in which precise articulation of claims, and rigorous argumentation could plausibly lead not only to a consensus, but to a vindicatory explanation of this consensus, we misunderstand the nature of ethical inquiry if we expect this.\""}
    +> [Middle-range rather than grand theory]
(4) [There is no final goal for ethical reflection]: While we can make progress by our own lights in ethical reflection, there is no final goal to which such attempts must tend. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 12", source: "\"While we can make progress by our own lights in ethical reflection, there is no final goal to which such attempts must tend.\""}
-----
(5) [General ethical theory may make judgement worse]: Deploying highly general ethical theories, which abstract from forms of local sense-making, may make our ethical judgement worse rather than better. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4, p. 12", source: "\"Deploying highly general ethical theories, which abstract from forms of local sense-making, may make our ethical judgement worse rather than better.\""}
    +> [Middle-range rather than grand theory]
